ADVSYS -- a sample adventure for advcom and advint==================================================RECOVERED 2026-08-22.  `advint' plays a compiled ADVSYS world and `advcom'compiles one, and this disk shipped neither a world nor anything to compile:there was no .adi and no .adv anywhere on it, so both programs had nothing todo.  These two files were still in the original archive, play/h4/ARR/advint.ar.    osample.adv    a very small sample adventure, by David Betz    objects.adi    the object definitions it includesTO RUN IT you need a shell with a real `chd', because of two limits in theprograms themselves:  * advcom holds a filename in 20 characters (FMAX in advint.h) and appends    `.adv' to it, so the name you give it must be 15 characters or fewer.    /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample is 24 and fails with "can't open input file".  * the `@objects.adi' include is opened relative to the DATA DIRECTORY, not    to the source file, so objects.adi has to be in the directory you are in.So:    chd /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS    advcom osample                  writes osample.dat    advint osample`bash' on this disk cannot do that -- its `cd' tracks the path as a string andnever moves the OS-9 data directory (see DOC/README-RUNNING).  On your ownOS-9 system, with a shell whose `chd' is real, it is three lines.advint.ar also carried an osample.dat, already compiled.  It is NOT here: thisadvint rejects it as "not an adventure data file", and the one advcom buildsfrom the same source is a different size.  It was made by another version, sothe source is the thing worth keeping.